          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "`nix develop` is the right tool here; `nix shell` is for one-off extra binaries.\n\n**What each does:**\n\n- `nix shell <installable>` puts the `bin/` of the named packages on `PATH` in a subshell. It sets no other variables, runs no `shellHook`, and gives you no build environment. With `nixpkgs#foo`, it resolves `nixpkgs` through your flake registry, not through this repo's `flake.lock`, so the version is whatever your registry points at today.\n- `nix develop` enters the flake's `devShells.default`, which is the build environment of a `mkShell` derivation. You get `PATH`, the stdenv toolchain (`cc`, `make`, and the variables that setup hooks such as pkg-config add), and the `shellHook`. Versions are pinned by `flake.lock`. `.envrc`'s `use flake` loads the same shell through direnv.\n\n**For llame:**\n\n- `flake.nix` exposes only `devShells.default` (`nodejs_22` + `pnpm_12`) and no `packages`, so `nix shell .` has nothing to install. `docs/development/runtime-and-package-manager.md:46` makes the dev shell the contract: `pnpm_12` from nixpkgs has to match `packageManager`, and an older global pnpm fails or silently runs as the wrong version.\n- The direnv hook does not fire in a non-interactive shell like my Bash tool or a CI-style script (moderate confidence for this setup). There, wrap the command explicitly: `nix develop -c pnpm lint` or `direnv exec . pnpm lint`.\n- `nix shell nixpkgs#…` is fine for a throwaway tool the dev shell doesn't carry, like the `nix shell nixpkgs#postgresql_17 --command …` in the archived model-prompts tasks. The cost is that it's unpinned and can drift from the lock. If a tool becomes something you need every week, add it to `mkShell.packages` instead.\n\nTwo cases where `nix develop` does more than `nix shell`: native addons that run node-gyp get a real C toolchain, and anything added to `shellHook` later only runs under `develop`.\n\nIf you want agents to keep using the right pnpm, add one line to `AGENTS.md`: run repo commands through `nix develop -c`. The runtime doc already states the rule but doesn't say how to apply it in a non-interactive shell.",
